WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.976 --> 00:00:04.464
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.887 --> 00:00:09.720
SOPHIE BEIERS: Hi, my
name is Sophie Beiers.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.490
Thank you for watching my video.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.860
I'm super honored to be a
part of this conference.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.930
I definitely wish that we
could all be in person.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.050
But in the meantime,
welcome to my kitchen.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.050
I am excited to talk to you
a little bit about my process

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.900
in creating data visualizations
and data driven stories

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.730
at the ACLU.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.420
So just a little bit about me.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.330
I'm a data journalist
at the ACLU

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.240
where I analyze data for
our lawyers' legal arguments

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.070
and visualize and
report on our data

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.380
for external advocacy purposes.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.660
I'm only a few years into being
a part of the R Community.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.340
So I'm really
excited to be here.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.720
So to start, what is data
science and data journalism

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.940
more broadly at the ACLU?

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.980
Some of you might
know and remember

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.730
Brooke Madubuonwu,
who spoke at the 2019 RStudio

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.950
Conference about her
data work in helping

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.440
reunify children that were
separated from their families

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.120
while seeking refuge
in our country.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.810
She happens to be my awesome
manager and colleague.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.410
And I felt she recently
summed up our work in just one

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.660
Slack that reads, the
lawyer called our analysis

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.450
both deeply disturbing
and extremely helpful

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.030
which might just be the
slogan for our team.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.060
This leads me to an
important word to define,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.150
terribulous, which is
commonly used on our team

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:24.680
as a way of
expressing excitement

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.620
over being able to prove
usually terrible news

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.660
with quantitative evidence.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.060
Our analysis of the data
that we receive often

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.340
uncovers disparities
that hold up in court

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.430
and help our lawyers win
their legal arguments.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.920
But in all seriousness,
what this often means

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.890
is the data that we are
privileged to analyze represent

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:48.000
real human beings who are being
harmed in one way or another.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.820
I'll briefly share
with you a few examples

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.190
of what this might mean.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.370
For example, each
row in this data

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.690
was a person that was
filed for eviction.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.900
We found in this case
that filings for eviction

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.630
impacted black women at
alarmingly disproportionate

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.920
rates.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.990
Another example is
this recent report

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.660
that we put out where we
found that black people,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.110
despite using marijuana at the
same rates as white people,

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.300
are still being
disproportionately arrested

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.800
for possession of marijuana.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.510
We also found that
people of color

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:23.630
were more likely to have their
driver's licenses suspended

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.930
due to unpaid and often
unaffordable fines and fees.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.170
And we were able to visualize
the story of two black women

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.410
whose lives were turned upside
down without the ability

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:34.160
to use their cars.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.850
All in all, I've been
really lucky to be

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.550
a part of these and other
data journalistic projects

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.440
during my time at the ACLU.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.170
So I wanted to talk to you a
little bit about the process

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.940
that I often go
through and the key

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.820
steps I take in creating
my data visualizations.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.940
This will include the
feedback that I sometimes

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:58.210
receive, what I've done or
not done with that feedback,

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.010
and other considerations
that I think

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.510
are important in getting
to a final result.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.990
So some of you might be thinking
that it's just this easy.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.340
You go from data analysis
to visualization.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.000
You're good.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.180
You have a great viz.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.140
And you can go on and
just enjoy your weekend.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.120
Maybe even I thought that at
one point based on this Slack

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.940
evidence when I
was analyzing data

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.000
few months into the pandemic
and came to the conclusion

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.430
that actually letting
incarcerated people out

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.330
of jails had no effect on
the crime levels nearby.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.610
Spoiler, this is
not the process,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.040
but it was the finding.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.500
The actual process
in my experience

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.622
can be really messy and
definitely not linear.

00:03:41.622 --> 00:03:44.080
But I'll share with you the
key elements and considerations

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.570
to take that I believe
have set me up for success

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.150
for projects like this.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:51.800
First, you'll get some data.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.260
A lot of the time
for us here that

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.450
means that a lawyer will
have some kind of data

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.850
that they've obtained
through the discovery process

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:00.860
or through a FOIA request.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:05.870
And a lot of times we'll just
use publicly available data.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.500
Next, you'll use R to read
and explore that data.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.270
During the
exploration process, I

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:13.430
use the Tidyverse
quite a bit.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.590
But also the janitor package
is one of my favorites

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:20.630
to make easy tables, and always
ggplot2 to find quick trends

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.030
and actually start
visualizing my findings.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.160
So this is important.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.033
By now, you'll want
to start thinking

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.700
about what are your
main points that you

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.350
found in your
exploration that you want

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:33.690
to visualize and make clear.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.390
Ask yourself, does my
visualization support

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:40.130
and highlight my main point?

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.490
You'll also want to carefully
consider your audience.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.530
Is your audience made
of purely academics?

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:46.658
Are they advocates?

00:04:46.658 --> 00:04:48.950
Are they people who know a
lot about your topic already

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:52.390
or maybe they know
nothing at all?

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:56.160
It's always also good to ask for
feedback on your visualizations

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.110
from various people.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.300
You'll want to be specific
about the main points

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.150
that you're trying to visualize
before receiving feedback

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.330
about whether those main points
are coming through clearly

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:09.500
or not to those people.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.350
And then you might find that,
once you receive feedback,

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.780
you'll start iterating
on your data viz, which

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.488
means you'll likely need
to go back and forth

00:05:17.488 --> 00:05:19.030
and check a lot of
these boxes again.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:25.850
The process could end up
looking a lot like this.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:28.160
Now, to show you how
I've gone about using

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.500
this process in practice,
I'll go through these steps

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.360
once more with a
real-life example.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.337
For the purposes of the
example that I'll use,

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:39.420
I'll need to start with a
little bit of background

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.150
on this recent project
that our team worked on.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.910
Sometime into the very beginning
of the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.530
it became really clear that
people in jails and people

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.810
that were incarcerated in
America, which by the way,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:55.200
has the highest incarceration
rate in the world,

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.110
in particular would be at risk.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.710
Incarcerated people are subject
to extreme overcrowding.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:05.040
And jails often lack the
resources and facilities

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.550
that allow for handwashing
and other hygienic practices

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.790
that are necessary to slow
the spread of COVID-19.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.520
So it was really clear that
in order to save lives,

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.050
action had to be taken.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.060
And it had to be taken quickly.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.340
Our team partnered with
academic researchers

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.610
to build a model that accounted
for the dynamics of the jail

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.970
system and estimated
the additional illnesses

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.620
and loss of life
that we'd expect

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.590
if none of these preventative
measures were taken.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:34.750
We recommended
reducing arrests such

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:36.580
that people were
only being arrested

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.270
for the most serious
incidents and promptly

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.360
releasing people who were
pre-trial, or charged

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.540
for low-level crimes, or
were particularly vulnerable

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:51.350
to the disease and posed no risk
of immediate threat to others.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:54.260
All in all, the team estimated
that taking these precautions

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.190
could save 100,000 human
lives both inside the jails

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.830
and in their
surrounding communities.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.725
Many communities did
begin prioritizing

00:07:02.725 --> 00:07:04.850
the lives of incarcerated
people and the people who

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.260
worked within jails.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.900
And some localities
reduced low level arrests,

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.240
or set bond at zero,
or released a subset

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.100
of incarcerated people.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.840
But there was some backlash.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.270
Some people were worried
that taking these actions

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.920
would result in a
huge spike in crime

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:20.570
in the surrounding communities.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:24.510
Despite research showing that,
in New York City for instance,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.690
people released from
jails due to COVID

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:30.660
were rearrested at lower
rates than even expected.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.670
To quell these concerns
further, our team

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.500
analyzed trends
in reported crimes

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.660
during the initial three
months of the pandemic

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.330
in 29 of the largest
US cities where

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:44.300
data was available and released
this report with our findings.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:45.980
For the purposes
of this project,

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:47.600
our goal was to
find whether there

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.450
was any relationship between
the change in county jail

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:54.170
populations and the reported
crime rate in the closest

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.870
metropolitan area.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:00.850
So back to the process,
starting with the data step,

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:04.090
we needed two categories of
data, one for county jail

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:06.940
populations and one that showed
the number of crimes reported

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.970
in the nearest city.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:11.790
These were, ultimately, the
29 locations for which I could

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.430
find quality reported
crime data for,

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.460
for the time periods necessary
that were either aggregated

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:22.200
daily, weekly, or monthly for
all of 2019 through May 2020

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.400
that we could also find jail
population information for.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:26.970
So we needed both.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.040
We collected this data
a bit in piecemeal,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.770
mainly through googling for
specific county sheriff's

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.890
offices or finding
public open data sets.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:37.320
We ended up downloading
countless CSVs and Excel

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.590
files and even PDFs
continuously whenever

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:44.010
we noticed a new bit
of data had dropped,

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.640
hence the circular arrow
around the data step.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.860
We also use the Vera
Institute's data

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.410
that tracked the number of
people in each county jail

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.460
over time.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.880
And we ended up using the
specific dates of February 29

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.670
and April 30 to
calculate the percent

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.830
change in jail population.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:04.710
Because most jails took action
early on in the pandemic,

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.560
we looked at the crime rates
during the time period of March

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.070
through May 2019 and compared
those to the same time

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.380
period in 2020.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.200
And then to standardize the
way that we analyzed crime,

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.590
and also because many of these
cities' crime data sets only

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:21.630
included these
types of crimes, we

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.170
focused on part one crimes
which are defined by the Uniform

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.060
Crime Reporting Program.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:29.880
And they encompass the most
frequently reported violent

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.710
and property crimes.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:35.790
You'll see that data exploration
here was super messy for me.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.730
This is an example of what
I saw using this function

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.040
from my very favorite
janitor package

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:43.590
on the crime description
column in just one city.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.430
During my exploration,
I created this plot in R

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:54.660
which showed the relationship
between the magnitude

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.630
of the change in jail
population and the magnitude

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:01.240
of the change in crime
during that same time frame.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.230
Now, it could be really tempting
to draw a regression line

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.980
and to try and focus on some
type of significant correlation

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.307
that just isn't there.

00:10:10.307 --> 00:10:11.890
I received feedback
from my colleagues

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.765
that this graph focused
a little bit too much

00:10:13.765 --> 00:10:17.400
on the outliers which were
one location that increased

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.400
their jail population
and one location where

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.750
there was more reported crime
than in the previous year.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.875
It was then I realized
that one of the most

00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:27.500
important main points
that I could make

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.190
was actually the lack of
relationship between x and y.

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:36.280
So after lots of data cleaning
and trying out more charts

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.815
and receiving more
feedback, these

00:10:37.815 --> 00:10:39.190
were the three
main points that I

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:40.900
wanted to make
clear in my report

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.900
and visualize to my audience.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:47.230
That, one, crime was not on the
rise compared to the same time

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.240
period the year prior.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:52.120
Crime rates were instead
right within normal range

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.800
or even lower.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.570
Second, most of the locales
that we found data for

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:59.020
did decrease their
jail populations

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:00.520
during this time period.

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.710
And like I said before,
just one location actually

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:04.840
increased its population.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.660
Lastly and importantly,
we found no relationship

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:10.870
between the county jail
population and its increase

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:15.400
or decrease and the number
of crimes that were reported.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.440
So once I decided on
these main points,

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.170
it was time to come up with
a few mockups of graphs

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.110
that I thought would
clearly express these.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.480
Of course, though, this
is not always easy,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:26.980
especially when you
realize that you

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.900
need to take your audience
into consideration.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.350
So unfortunately,
this is an example

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:34.700
of a time where I
thought I nailed it.

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:36.910
I had come to the
perfect solution.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:39.890
But I hadn't yet
considered our audience.

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:41.680
So this is a graph
that shows the 29

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:43.210
locations in order
of the percent

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.430
change in jail population.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:48.040
The gray bar indicates the
range of monthly crimes

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:51.370
between January 2018
and February 2020.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:53.950
And the three dots indicate
the number of monthly crimes

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:57.190
reported in March,
April, and May of 2020.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.500
You'll see that a lot
of the time the three

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.030
dots are to the
left of the gray bar

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:04.120
but never are they to
the right of the gray bar

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.920
which indicates
that reported crime

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.530
during these first few
months of the pandemic

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:12.490
were within normal or
expected range or lower.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:15.700
I will give credit to Lucia,
or chief analytics officer,

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:18.590
for scribbling down this
idea, in Slack, of course,

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:20.860
but will give credit
to myself for what

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:24.770
I thought was kind of
incredible execution.

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:28.100
The team of data scientists,
of course, loved it.

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:30.460
I thought I was basically done.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:34.130
But let's remember,
your audience is key.

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:36.340
So the next day I received
the following feedback

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:39.460
from several
non-analytics folks.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:41.290
It honestly was a
little bit like reading

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.780
my own bad reviews.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:46.330
But it's important not to
look at constructive feedback

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:47.620
that way.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:50.350
These colleagues were speaking
up about some major issues

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:51.680
with this graph.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:52.600
You might agree.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.220
I agree now.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:55.480
They were right.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:58.120
I see feedback that's qualified
with things like, I'm not

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:01.210
a numbers person, a lot.

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:03.160
People sometimes
blame themselves

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.470
for not knowing how to
interpret a data viz.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.780
But it's actually really
our job to make sure

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:11.480
that the viz works for them
and works for our audience.

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.600
So this crimes and
decarceration report

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.550
was not just for
data scientists.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:17.920
It was for a far more
general audience.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:20.810
And this chart was
clearly not working.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:24.170
So sometimes this means going
back to the drawing board,

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.270
doing a little bit
more data exploration,

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:29.780
always with the janitor
and ggplot packages for me,

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:32.480
and trying out new
ways of visualizing

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:35.900
your main points while
thinking about your audience.

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:38.690
These charts where
hashtag innoventive,

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:40.760
but they simply weren't working.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.190
Some of the most valuable
feedback conversations I had

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:44.180
were not over Slack.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:45.830
So I have no hard evidence.

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:47.840
But in the times that
you're asking for feedback

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.670
from someone, it's
really important

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:53.870
to ask the why behind something
isn't working for them.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:57.770
Sometimes the why might
be sort of surprising,

00:13:57.770 --> 00:13:59.840
or maybe it'll make
you actually realize

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:02.960
that what you need to do is
add better annotating rather

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.790
than just scrapping the whole
thing and starting over.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:10.330
And in the spirit of reiterating
that this process was one messy

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.630
adventure, before showing you
the final visualizations we

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:15.830
ended up with, at
the end of the day,

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:20.380
I really feel like this quote
just drives it all home.

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.500
So these were the final graphics
that we published on our site.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:27.040
Both were created in R and
touched up in Illustrator.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.200
Both, I feel, do a good
job of expressing our three

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:33.350
main points and are not just
interpretable for quote,

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.230
"numbers people."

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:37.850
I know these are probably
a little bit hard to see.

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:40.670
So I'll share a link to my
report in my presentation

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.270
as well as where you can find
the R code for the analysis

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.560
and viz.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.950
And one last wrench
I'll throw at you,

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.380
because it was thrown at
me, was that it turns out

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:54.050
police data is often dynamic,
meaning that sometimes crimes

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:55.550
that occurred
actually maybe a month

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.310
ago could have been
reported today.

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:01.550
I cannot stress enough how much
of a life saver reusable code

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:04.850
in R was throughout this process
given the amount of extremely

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:08.930
last-minute data dumps and the
changing out the CSV files here

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:10.080
and there.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.280
We wanted to be precise.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:13.820
And thanks to R, that remaking
these graphs time and time

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:17.410
again was such a small deal.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:19.330
So hopefully,
through this process,

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:22.420
I've been able to convince
you that data viz is

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:24.640
and should often be non-linear.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:26.050
Mentors in my life
have taught me

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:28.810
that being good at making
effective visualizations

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.960
is not about getting from point
A to point B the quickest.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:33.940
It's actually about
carefully considering

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.892
each of these points that makes
you an effective communicator

00:15:36.892 --> 00:15:37.600
to your audience.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:43.970
Lastly, feel free to reach out
to me if you have any questions

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:47.840
or just want to nerd out
about data viz, and thank you.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:56.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

